    "result_text": "Tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**Agreed with the main finding** \u2014 the Soundex encoder was a pure, stateless `String\u2192String` algorithm that had no business living on the JPA `@Entity`, especially once the controller was reaching into `Owner` for string math across layers.\n\n- **Created `util/Soundex.java`** \u2014 an abstract static-helper class (matching the existing `EntityUtils` idiom in that package) exposing `Soundex.encode(String)`, with the mapping table and the private H/W-rule helper moved wholesale. The algorithm is unchanged; only its home moved.\n- **`Owner.getIdentityKey()`** now calls `Soundex.encode(...)`; the ~75 lines of Soundex code are gone from the entity, leaving it focused on owner state and owner-specific derivations.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.flagPossibleDuplicate`** now calls `Soundex.encode(...)` directly instead of reaching through `Owner`, so the web layer depends on the util, not the entity.\n- Updated imports and the `{@linkplain}` javadoc references accordingly.\n\n**Left the minor/optional finding alone (with reasoning):** I kept `sha256HexLower(String)` private on `Owner`. It's used only by `getIdentityKey()` and sits next to the pre-existing `sha256Hex8` helper that backs the customer code. Extracting a one-method `Hashing` util would split SHA-256-hex logic across two places while a near-identical helper stayed on the entity \u2014 net fragmentation, not consolidation. The review explicitly flagged this as defensible and \"not required on its own,\" so I left it.\n\nAll acceptance behaviour is intact: identity key is still the 64-char lowercase SHA-256 hex, duplicate detection still 409s on an exact key match, and the soft-match still keys on Soundex + postcode with a differing identity key.",
